<header><p class="kicker">Hydrodynamics · Module assessment</p>
<h1>OrcaWave utilities: spectral response assessment</h1>
<p>Draft for maintainer review</p></header>
<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>The OrcaWave utilities module is a collection of frequency-domain hydrodynamic calculation and reporting functions. Analysis is performed to determine the numerical response of a documented synthetic example; key results are summarized below.</p>
<ul><li>The spectral calculation can be reproduced without a diffraction solution.
<ul><li>The response variance is {{m0}} m², compared with {{reference_variance}} m² for the full-frequency analytic reference. The difference is {{variance_difference_percent}}% on the supplied finite-frequency grid.</li></ul></li>
<li>An operating decision is not established by this example.
<ul><li>The response operator is synthetic. An operating limit is not supplied, and physical performance is not evaluated.</li></ul></li></ul>
<h3>Revision history</h3>
<table><thead><tr><th>Revision</th><th>Date</th><th>What changed and why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A</td><td>2026-10-21</td><td>Initial assessment: the spectral calculation, its analytic comparator and its qualification limits are presented.</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>2026-10-22</td><td>Equation citations, independent numerical checks, display precision and the bandwidth qualification clarify the calculation basis and its limits.</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Table R. Revision history.</p>
<details><summary>Engineering changes</summary><ul id="changes"></ul></details>
<h2>2. Introduction</h2>
<p>The purpose, scope and status of the assessment are presented in this section. The main workflow is the conversion of a wave spectrum and a response-amplitude operator into a response spectrum and short-term motion statistics.</p>
<p>The response-amplitude operator relates motion amplitude to incident wave amplitude at each frequency. The example is reproduced from a public software test, not from a measured or solved hull. The commercial diffraction application and the Python utilities are distinct: no diffraction solution is required by the calculation presented here.</p>
<h3>2.1 Inputs and outputs</h3>
<p>The calculation accepts an increasing angular-frequency array, aligned nonnegative wave spectral densities and finite response amplitudes. Translational amplitudes are expressed in metres per metre. The outputs are the response spectrum, spectral moments, root-mean-square motion, significant single and double amplitudes, zero-crossing period and narrow-band maximum estimates. Rotational response requires a declared angular unit; it is not included in this example.</p>
<h3>2.2 Not covered</h3>
<p>The module also contains response-table interpolation and comparison, dimensional hydrodynamic coefficient exchange, panel geometry checks, drift forces, long-term statistics, motion-sickness estimates, synthetic hull response data and result-file reporting. Their interfaces are listed in the reference records. They are not qualified by this spectral example. Native result loading, diffraction, mesh convergence and body interaction are not evaluated.</p>
<h2>3. Design Data</h2>
<p>The example data are taken from the public significant-response test identified in Section 9.2. The response operator is constant across frequency and represents an idealised translational response, not a particular hull.</p>
<p>The significant incident wave height is {{hs}} m and the peak period is {{tp}} s. The response-amplitude operator is {{rao}} m/m. The angular-frequency grid contains {{points}} points between {{omega_min}} and {{omega_max}} rad/s. The duration is {{duration}} s, taken from the documented statistics-function default.</p>
<p>ASSUMED - to be confirmed: the constant operator is interpreted as vertical translation solely to explain the units. No geometry, displacement, water depth, heading, mooring restraint or damping has been supplied for a physical body.</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 760 240" role="img" aria-label="Idealised vertical response schematic; no physical geometry or mesh supplied" style="width:100%">
<path d="M20 125 Q45 95 70 125 T120 125 T170 125 T220 125 T270 125 M490 125 Q515 95 540 125 T590 125 T640 125 T690 125 T740 125" fill="none" stroke="var(--brand)" stroke-width="2"/>
<path d="M280 95 L470 95 L445 158 L305 158 Z" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
<path d="M375 40 V200 M367 50 L375 40 L383 50 M367 190 L375 200 L383 190" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)"/>
<text x="25" y="65">Incident surface elevation η</text><text x="400" y="48">Vertical response z</text>
