<h2>4. Analysis Methodology</h2>
<p>The implemented spectral relations are evaluated on the supplied angular-frequency grid. The equations below describe the code identified in Section 9.2; they do not assert conformity with a design standard.</p>
<h3>4.1 Spectrum and response</h3>
<div class="eq">ωₚ = 2π/Tₚ<br>Sη(ω) = (5/16)Hₛ²ωₚ⁴ω⁻⁵ exp[−(5/4)(ωₚ/ω)⁴]<br>Sz(ω) = |R(ω)|² Sη(ω)<br>mₙ = ∫ ωⁿ Sz(ω) dω</div>
<p>Here ω is angular frequency in rad/s, ωₚ is peak angular frequency, Tₚ is peak period in seconds, Hₛ is significant wave height in metres, R is the response-amplitude operator, and Sη and Sz are incident-wave and response spectral densities in m²·s/rad. The moment order is n. The coefficients follow DNV-RP-C205 (2014), Section 3.5.5, JONSWAP spectrum with peak enhancement γ = 1, which gives the modified Pierson–Moskowitz spectrum (citation record W2). The integral is evaluated by the trapezoidal rule over the supplied range.</p>
<h3>4.2 Statistics and comparison basis</h3>
<div class="eq">σ = √m₀; Aₛ = 2σ; Hₛ,z = 4σ<br>Tz = 2π√(m₀/m₂); ε = √[1 − m₂²/(m₀m₄)]<br>N = D/Tz; xmode ≈ σ√(2 ln N)<br>E[xmax] ≈ xmode + γE σ²/xmode</div>
<p>The symbols σ, Aₛ and Hₛ,z denote root-mean-square motion, significant single amplitude and significant double amplitude, all in metres. Tz is zero-crossing period, ε is spectral bandwidth, D is duration, N is the cycle count and γE is the Euler–Mascheroni constant. The maximum expressions are narrow-band Rayleigh/Gumbel asymptotes; citation record R1 derives them from the Rayleigh peak law through the exponential transformation and square-root expansion. They require more than one cycle and are not broadband certification criteria.</p>
<p>The significant-height relation Hₛ = 4√m₀ gives the full-frequency wave variance Hₛ²/16 for the spectrum in DNV-RP-C205 (2014), Section 3.5.5, with γ = 1 (W2). For a constant operator, multiplication by R² gives the analytic response variance (R Hₛ/4)². The relative variance difference is defined as 100(m₀/reference − 1)%. This comparator exposes grid truncation; no acceptance tolerance is assigned to that difference. The spectrum has a nonnegative-density mathematical bound. A physical motion limit is not supplied and is not evaluated.</p>
<h2>5. Detailed Results</h2>
<p>The module returns the values below for the public synthetic example. All example values and chart ordinates are calculated during report generation from the same inputs.</p>
<p>The root-mean-square response is {{rms}} m. The significant single amplitude is {{significant_single_amplitude}} m and the significant double amplitude is {{significant_double_amplitude}} m. These quantities are different and are not interchangeable.</p>
<p>The zero-crossing period is {{mean_zero_crossing_period}} s, the spectral bandwidth is {{spectral_bandwidth}}, and the duration contains approximately {{cycles}} cycles. The most probable maximum estimate is {{most_probable_maximum_3hr}} m; the expected maximum asymptote is {{expected_maximum_3hr}} m. This computed bandwidth is not narrow-band: ε is not close to the narrow-band limit of zero. The Rayleigh/Gumbel maxima are therefore approximate numerical comparisons; their physical applicability is not established.</p>
<p>The response spectral density is plotted against angular frequency. The dashed line is the nonnegative-density bound, not an operating limit. Hover over the curve or use the point selector to inspect the case, component, demand, bound and units.</p>
<figure><svg id="spectrum" viewBox="0 0 760 350" style="width:100%" role="img" aria-label="Synthetic vertical response spectrum with zero-density bound"></svg>
<label>Frequency point <input id="point" type="range" min="0" value="0"></label>
<p id="readout" aria-live="polite"></p><figcaption>Figure B. Response spectral density and mathematical lower bound.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>5.1 Existing validation evidence</h3>
<p>The public spectrum tests check nonnegativity, approximate height recovery, peak placement and spectral-shape relationships. The response tests check spectrum preservation for a unit operator and zero response for a zero operator. Separate equation tests cover the distinction between significant amplitudes and the Rayleigh domain. These are numerical checks, not measured-body validation.</p>
<p>The equation record includes independently integrated spectrum comparisons and analytical response limits. The reporting fixtures use simulated result objects. Neither those fixtures nor the synthetic response database establish agreement with a native diffraction result. A public hydrodynamics verification page provides an adjacent wave-excited-body example, but it does not exercise this constant-operator calculation and its results are not transferred here.</p>
<h2>6. Key Findings</h2>
<p>The calculation supports numerical inspection of a supplied spectrum and response operator. Physical motion acceptance remains dependent on evidence outside this example.</p>
<ul><li>The finite-frequency result can be compared with an analytic reference.<ul><li>The variance difference includes truncation and quadrature effects; it is not a measured model error.</li></ul></li>
<li>The statistical outputs have distinct meanings.<ul><li>Single and double significant amplitudes are reported separately. Maximum estimates retain the narrow-band qualification.</li></ul></li></ul>
<h2>7. Limitations</h2>
<p>The principal uncertainties are in the response operator and the applicability of the peak distribution. Neither is resolved by reproduction of the numerical example.</p>
<p>The constant response operator contains no resonance, phase, heading dependence or viscous damping. Hull-specific motion cannot be calculated from it.</p>
<p>The finite grid excludes spectral tails. The higher moments and derived bandwidth are particularly sensitive to the upper frequency bound; the reported maximum estimates are approximate.</p>
<p>Measured response, native-output agreement, mesh convergence and operating-limit compliance are NOT_EVALUATED. No failing physical operating result is asserted because no physical acceptance criterion is supplied.</p>
<h2>8. Recommendations</h2>
<p>The recommended qualification sequence follows the input and statistical requirements in Section 4 and the implementation records in Section 9.2. Acceptance is conditional on documented physical inputs and a declared motion criterion.</p>
<p>Replace the synthetic operator with a qualified response table carrying units, heading, reference point and phase convention before using the statistics for an engineering decision. Repeat integration over a wider and finer frequency grid and compare each governing response quantity against an agreed numerical tolerance.</p>
<p>Establish a motion limit and the associated duration before assessing an operating envelope. Where the response is broadband, compare the Rayleigh maximum approximation with a suitable independent response history or peak-distribution model before accepting an extreme estimate.</p>
<h2>9. References</h2>
<p>The calculation basis and evidence are identified below. Implementation records support reproduction; they are not substituted for licensed standards or physical qualification.</p>
<h3>9.1 Basis</h3>
<p>The spectrum basis is <a href="https://www.dnv.com/energy/standards-guidelines/dnv-rp-c205-environmental-conditions-and-environmental-loads/">DNV-RP-C205, Environmental conditions and environmental loads</a>, April 2014, Section 3.5.5, JONSWAP spectrum, with γ = 1 (citation record W2). This edition-specific citation does not assert conformity with a later edition. Citation record R1 supplies the mathematical derivation of the Rayleigh/Gumbel maximum estimates. The citation records are identified in Section 9.2. No vendor-licensed document is reproduced, and no physical limit is introduced.</p>
<h3>9.2 Source records and reproduction</h3>
<ul class="refs">
<li>Public example: <code>tests/orcawave/test_motion_statistics.py</code>, <code>TestShortTermStatistics.test_significant_response</code>; grid, height, period and response amplitude.</li>
<li>Spectrum equation and quadrature: <code>src/digitalmodel/orcawave/wave_spectrum.py</code>, <code>pierson_moskowitz</code> and <code>compute_spectral_moments</code>.</li>
<li>Statistics and duration: <code>src/digitalmodel/orcawave/motion_statistics.py</code>, <code>compute_response_spectrum</code> and <code>short_term_statistics</code>.</li>
<li>Equation derivation: <code>src/digitalmodel/orcawave/docs/engineering-citations.md</code>, response-statistics derivation and spectrum comparisons. Current implementation is used where earlier inventory prose differs.</li>
<li>Numerical checks: <code>tests/orcawave/test_wave_spectrum.py</code>, <code>test_motion_statistics.py</code>, <code>test_rayleigh_domain.py</code> and <code>test_audit_p1.py</code>.</li>
<li>Module interfaces: <code>src/digitalmodel/orcawave/__init__.py</code>. Simulated reporting evidence: <code>tests/orcawave/conftest.py</code>.</li>
<li>Adjacent public example and layout exemplar: <code>docs/api/cfd/wave-excited-body-rao-verification.html</code>; common stylesheet: <code>docs/api/_assets/brand.css</code>.</li>
<li>Reproduction: with the repository source on the Python import path, execute <code>python docs/reports/modules/orcawave/build_report.py</code>. Numerical dependencies are NumPy and Pydantic. The reproducibility check is <code>tests/orcawave/test_module_report.py</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h2>10. Acronyms</h2>
<p>The abbreviations used by the module are defined here. Angular frequency is distinguished from frequency in cycles per second throughout the calculation.</p>
<p>RAO means response-amplitude operator. RMS means root mean square. The mathematical symbols are defined beside their equations in Section 4. NOT_EVALUATED means that evidence required for an assessment is absent; it does not mean zero demand or compliance.</p>
<h2>Appendix A. Data register and numerical evidence</h2>
<p>The retained input and output arrays are embedded in this document. Source digests of UTF-8 text with normalized line endings identify only the two calculation source files. Test files, citation prose and layout assets are excluded from those digests. Full-document regeneration still depends on the inlined stylesheet and layout exemplar; a presentation change requires regenerating the report. Embedded numerical data are compared with relative tolerance 10⁻¹² and zero absolute tolerance to allow platform roundoff; the remaining document content and source digests are compared exactly.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Readiness</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Wave height, peak period, grid and constant response amplitude</td><td>Public synthetic calculation inputs; no physical design status</td><td>Section 9.2 public example</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical-translation interpretation</td><td>ASSUMED - to be confirmed</td><td>Section 3 explanatory assumption</td></tr>
<tr><td>Geometry, mesh, heading and damping</td><td>Not supplied; physical response is NOT_EVALUATED</td><td>Section 7 limitations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motion limit and numerical tolerance</td><td>Required inputs for acceptance</td><td>Sections 4.2 and 8</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Table A. Input readiness and intended use.</p>
